---
import Learn from "../layouts/Learn.astro";
import * as CH from "./inflation.charts.js";
const NUM = CH.NUM;
---
<Learn
  title="Inflation is not “prices going up” · India Atlas"
  description="What inflation actually is: a rate of change, not a price. Falling inflation is not falling prices; the price level almost never returns. Explained with a decade of real CPI data."
  slug="inflation"
  kicker="Concepts: Inflation"
  h1="Falling inflation is not <i>falling prices.</i>"
  lede="The most misread number in the economy, taken apart once and properly: what inflation is, what it is not, and how to read any print without being fooled."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "Inflation"],
    ["Illustrated with", "CPI combined, 2016–2025"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "9 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Inflation"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Every month, the government's statisticians check the price of roughly three hundred things an ordinary household buys: rice, milk, bus fares, haircuts, school fees. They compare the total with the same month a year earlier, and the percentage change they report is inflation. When you hear "inflation is 4 per cent", it means the basket costs <b>4 per cent more than it did twelve months ago</b>. Nothing more, and nothing less.</p>
        <p>Two ideas are doing all the work in that sentence, and they are worth separating, because most confusion about inflation is really confusion between them: <b>what things cost</b>, and <b>how fast that cost is rising</b>.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Inflation <span class="sub">a rate, reported monthly</span></div>
          <p>The percentage change in the price of a fixed basket of goods and services over the past twelve months. If the basket cost ₹1,000 a year ago and costs ₹1,040 today, inflation is 4 per cent.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> it answers one question only: how fast is life getting more expensive?</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">Notice what the definition does not say. It does not say things are expensive. The basket could be cheap and getting costlier quickly, or expensive and getting costlier slowly. Inflation is a speed, not a height. The height has its own name.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Price level <span class="sub">a stock, what the basket costs</span></div>
          <p>What the basket actually costs at a point in time, shown as an index rather than in rupees. This site uses 2012 as the base, so 2012 = 100. A level of 198 means the basket costs 98 per cent more than it did in 2012.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> it answers the other question: how expensive is life, full stop?</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">A car easing off the accelerator is still moving forward. Inflation falling is still prices rising; only reverse gear, a negative print, means prices genuinely fell, and reverse gear is rare. Three words cover every configuration the news will ever report. They are not interchangeable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>Falling inflation ≠ falling prices.</b> The price level almost never returns. A falling rate is slower pain, not relief.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>The word for cheap is cheap.</b> Inflation says nothing about the level of prices. A low-inflation city can still be ruinously expensive.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>One number, two questions.</b> “How expensive is life?” is the level. “How fast is it getting worse?” is inflation. The news answers the second and lets you assume the first.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="break ledger" style="margin-top:56px">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain meaning</th><th>The price level</th><th>What you feel</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Inflation<small>the rate is positive</small></td>
            <td class="name">Prices rising, quickly or slowly</td>
            <td class="chg">Rising</td>
            <td class="chg">Life getting costlier</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Disinflation<small>the rate is falling, still positive</small></td>
            <td class="name">Prices rising more slowly than before</td>
            <td class="chg">Still rising</td>
            <td class="chg">The pain is slowing, not reversing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Deflation<small>the rate is negative</small></td>
            <td class="name">Prices actually falling</td>
            <td class="chg">Falling</td>
            <td class="chg">Genuinely cheaper; rare, and not always good news</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 plate 1 : the level and the rate -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>Two drawings of the same decade</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Index level, y/y %</span></div>
    <p>The two ideas draw two very different pictures of the same ten years. Below is the decade drawn twice: once as the level, what the basket cost month by month, and once as the rate, the form in which inflation reaches the news.</p>
    <aside class="defbox">
      <div class="term">Price index <span class="sub">base 2012 = 100</span></div>
      <p>A way of showing the price level without rupees. Statisticians fix the basket's cost in a chosen base year at 100 and measure every other month against it. A reading of 198 means things cost 98 per cent more than in the base year, with no currency arithmetic needed.</p>
      <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the base year is a ruler's zero mark, not a claim that things were cheap then.</div>
    </aside>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-lr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The price level climbs while the inflation rate falls: both true at once</h3>
            <div class="sub">CPI combined, above: index level, 2012 = 100, below: year-on-year rate, Jan 2016 to Dec 2025</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-lr" set:html={NUM["pv-lr"]}></div><div class="c">Dec 2025, index level</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-level" set:html={CH.level()} />
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-rate" style="margin-top:34px" set:html={CH.rateChart()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, CPI group indices, base 2012=100, vintage <span id="pf-lr" set:html={NUM["pf-lr"]}></span>, shaded band: RBI tolerance 2–6%, dotted: 4% target</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The top line is what things cost; the bottom line is how fast that cost is rising. The bottom is only the top measured against twelve months back, so every wiggle below has a parent above. When the bottom line falls, the top line merely flattens. A dip needs more: only a negative reading below turns the top line down, and that is deflation.</p>
            <p>The level does dip month to month; it did thirty-one times in this decade, mostly in winter. But a dip is a pause, not a return. Compared with a year earlier, the basket has cost more in every single month of this series. The highest reading on the top line is the last one.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2025 case</span>
            <p>Through 2025 the rate collapsed, from <b id="lr-rjan" set:html={NUM["lr-rjan"]}></b> in January to <b id="lr-rdec" set:html={NUM["lr-rdec"]}></b> in December, the calmest year-end in the series. And the level? It wobbled through the winter, as it does, then climbed to <b id="lr-ldec" set:html={NUM["lr-ldec"]}></b>, the highest reading the index has ever recorded.</p>
            <p>Inflation fell all year. Prices rose all year. Both statements are true at once, and holding them together is the whole lesson of this page.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="defbox" style="margin-top:44px">
          <div class="term">Tolerance band <span class="sub">RBI, 2% to 6%, since 2016</span></div>
          <p>The range within which the Reserve Bank of India must keep inflation. If the rate spends three straight quarters above 6 per cent or below 2 per cent, the RBI must write to the government explaining what went wrong and what it plans to do. The 4 per cent in the middle is the target.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> on the chart it is the shaded strip: law, not decoration.</div>
        </aside>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 the arithmetic -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>The arithmetic the headline compresses</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">A worked example, Dec 2024 → Dec 2025</span></div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The basket, Dec 2024</div><div class="v" id="n-lvl-then" set:html={NUM["n-lvl-then"]}></div><div class="d">index points, 2012 = 100</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The basket, Dec 2025</div><div class="v" id="n-lvl-now" set:html={NUM["n-lvl-now"]}></div><div class="d">index points, 2012 = 100</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The rate, Dec 2025</div><div class="v acc" id="n-rate-now" set:html={NUM["n-rate-now"]}></div><div class="d">the ratio of the two, minus one</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">What ₹1,000 became</div><div class="v" id="n-rupee" set:html={NUM["n-rupee"]}></div><div class="d">same basket, one year later</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">The index is not money, but its ratio is. Divide the December 2025 level by the December 2024 level and you get the print: <b id="ar-ratio" set:html={NUM["ar-ratio"]}></b> over <b id="ar-then" set:html={NUM["ar-then"]}></b> is 1.013: the 1.3 per cent of the headline. A basket that cost ₹1,000 in December 2024 cost <b id="ar-rupee" set:html={NUM["ar-rupee"]}></b> a year later. The rate can halve while the total climbs, because the rate only ever measures the climb.</p>
    <aside class="defbox">
      <div class="term">Year-on-year <span class="sub">y/y, the standard comparison</span></div>
      <p>A comparison with the same month a year earlier: December against December, not against November. Comparing like with like cancels the seasons: mangoes are dear every summer and cheap every winter, so month-on-month moves are mostly weather and festivals. What remains in the year-on-year figure is real change.</p>
      <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> this is the measure the news means when it says "the inflation rate".</div>
    </aside>
    <p style="margin-top:1.4em">And the climb compounds. Each year's print is applied to a level already raised by every print before it, which is why a decade of mostly moderate inflation has made the basket <b id="ar-decade" set:html={NUM["ar-decade"]}></b> more expensive since 2016, with no year of relief subtracted anywhere in between.</p>
    <aside class="defbox">
      <div class="term">Compounding <span class="sub">growth on growth</span></div>
      <p>Each year's inflation is applied to a level already raised by every year before it. Ten years of 5 per cent inflation does not make the basket 50 per cent more expensive. It makes it about 63 per cent more expensive, because each year's 5 per cent multiplies all the increases that came before.</p>
      <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> this is why a decade of "moderate" inflation quietly doubles prices.</div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 plate 2 : actual deflation -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>What falling prices actually look like</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Index level, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-foodlvl" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Deflation is the level line turning down: food did it, briefly</h3>
            <div class="sub">Food &amp; beverages, index level, 2012 = 100, Jan 2023 to Dec 2025</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-foodlvl" set:html={NUM["pv-foodlvl"]}></div><div class="c">Dec 2025, index level</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-foodlvl" set:html={CH.foodLevel()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, CPI food &amp; beverages group index, base 2012=100, <span id="pf-foodlvl" set:html={NUM["pf-foodlvl"]}></span></span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is a level chart, not a rate chart. It is the same view as the top line of the previous plate, zoomed to three years. Deflation lives here: not the line flattening, not the rate falling, but the line itself turning down. A peak, then a genuine decline. Anything less is disinflation wearing deflation's clothes.</p>
          </div>          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2025 case</span>
            <p>The food index peaked in October 2024 at <b id="fl-peak" set:html={NUM["fl-peak"]}></b> and slid to <b id="fl-now" set:html={NUM["fl-now"]}></b> by December 2025, about <b id="fl-drop" set:html={NUM["fl-drop"]}></b> cheaper at the shelf. Vegetables drove the fall, reversing the spike of the autumn before. That is what falling prices look like: food is the only major group that does it with any regularity. Relief at the shop and distress on the farm, in the same figure.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="defbox" style="margin-top:44px">
          <div class="term">Deflation <span class="sub">the rate below zero</span></div>
          <p>An actual fall in the price level: the basket cheaper than a year ago, not merely rising more slowly. In India it happens mostly in food, and mostly after a spike, when a glut follows a shortage. Sustained deflation across the whole basket is rare, and it is not a gift: falling prices reward waiting over spending.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> a short food dip is relief; a lasting fall in everything is an economy holding its breath.</div>
        </aside>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Inflation fell, so prices fell.”</h3>
          <p>No. Falling inflation means prices are rising more slowly: <b>disinflation</b>. Only a negative print means falling prices, and negative headline prints are rare enough to count on one hand in a decade.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Low inflation means things are cheap.”</h3>
          <p>No. Inflation measures change, never level. Two per cent inflation in Zurich and in Patna report the same speed and say nothing about cost. <b>The word for cheap is cheap</b>. Use it instead.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“The rate going down means my bill went down.”</h3>
          <p>Your bill went up by less. The print is a ratio to a year ago. <b>1.3 per cent of what?</b> Of last year's level, which itself sits on every increase of the decade before it. Inflation compounds; the level carries the full history.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Deflation would be a gift.”</h3>
          <p>Be careful what you wish for. Falling prices reward waiting: why buy today what will be cheaper next month? Held long enough, that pause stalls wages, hiring and credit. A short food-led dip is relief; a general, lasting fall in prices is an economy holding its breath.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Consumer Price Index group indices on base 2012=100, rural, urban and combined; retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage 2026-08-04. Levels shown are the published indices; the year-on-year rate is computed from them.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Inflation here means the year-on-year change in the CPI combined index, the measure the RBI's 2–6 per cent tolerance band applies to. Month-on-month changes exist and are noisier; the year-on-year form is what the news calls "the inflation rate".</p>
    <p>Where the data cannot answer a question, this page says so rather than inventing precision.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Concepts, version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. The 2025 episode is used as history, not news; the charts redraw from the baked data on every build.</p>
    <p>Companion guide: <a href="/guides/cpi/">Consumer Price Index</a>.</p>
  </div>

</Learn>
